 "note": "Cloud A/B on GitHub's Linux runner: ubuntu-latest, 4 CPUs, no GPU, so WebGPU runs on SwiftShader. Two of pull request #21's own runs on 2026-10-12, each an A/A in effect: the base is main (c1b54913ac) and the head the A/B's own branch, which adds the harness both arms share and changes no renderer code. The runner's artifacts cannot be downloaded from a cloud session, so every figure here is read from the job logs, which print each round's totals; those are here in full, as [base, head] per round, the sum of the arm's two frames. run6 was cancelled by the next push after four shots, the fourth at 5 of its 16 rounds, so it has no report: its estimates are the Hodges-Lehmann estimates of those rounds. run7 is the first complete run of the gating job on GitHub: no regression, GPU time SAME on all five shots, and draw calls, work counts and pixels identical. One round's GPU-time ratio scattered by 0.10-0.33%, against 3.7-4.1% in a cloud session, so each GPU interval is a few tenths of a per cent wide, and all five of run7's exclude zero: the head's GPU time sat -0.2% to +0.8% from the base's, an offset between the run's two browsers that changed from one run to the other (approach-500ft +0.66% in run6, +0.20% in run7). CPU time read the head 2-7% slower on run6's four shots and 0-7% faster on run7's five, each of run7's intervals inside the 15% guard. A frame cost 6.6-16 s of GPU time on run6's runner and 9.4-20 s on run7's, so runners differ in speed by about a third. run7's A/B step took 8,993 s, about two and a half hours, for five shots of 20-36 minutes each, which is why the workflow now runs them as five parallel parts. docs/PERFORMANCE.md, Cloud A/B, reads these.",
